<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish protectorate in Morocco</h1><p class="st-meta">1912 CE – 1956 CE · capital Tetouan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's zone of Morocco, covering the Rif and the north coast, scene of the Rif War and the launchpad of Franco's 1936 coup.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fez">Treaty of Fez</a> created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-protectorate-in-morocco">French protectorate in Morocco</a> in 1912, a Franco-Spanish treaty of November 1912 assigned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> a northern zone along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, including the Rif and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jbala">Jbala</a> mountains, and a southern zone around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-juby">Cape Juby</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a> became the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan was represented by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">khalifa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a>, while a Spanish high commissioner held real power. The mountain tribes resisted fiercely: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a> and the disaster at Annual in 1921 cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> thousands of lives. Spain later recruited Moroccan troops, and in July 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> launched his rising from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armee-d-afrique">Army of Africa</a> to the mainland.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Following French recognition of Moroccan independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ended its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in April 1956, and the northern zone joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarfaya">Tarfaya</a> strip followed in 1958. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melilla">Melilla</a> remained Spanish.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-protectorate-in-morocco.jpg" alt="Spanish protectorate in Morocco" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Xiquet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merchant_flag_of_Spanish_Morocco.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1912 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tetouan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moulay Hassan ben el-Mehdi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber, Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Spanish Morocco, Protectorado español de Marruecos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=spanish-protectorate-in-morocco" aria-label="Spanish protectorate in Morocco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4544 892 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish protectorate in Morocco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4864 1148L4909 989M4864 1148L4909 989M4864 1148L4909 989M4864 1148L4858 1205M4864 1148L4901 1161"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 989h0M4909 989h0M4909 989h0M4858 1205h0M4901 1161h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4864 1148h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4864 1148h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tetouan. <a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=spanish-protectorate-in-morocco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
